 in  a water tank and susie davis and her  team  the designer and her team had to  find a way to sort of lower this set  uh into the water  you know the one thing i'll say about  benedict is that you know aside from  being an extremely versatile gifted  actor he's also  incredibly courageous  and sort of fearless  and so i was really impressed by how he  was performing even to the point of  being fully submerged  there is a moment where because of the  the level of emotion of of crying and  collapsing underwater i'm letting out a  lot of air a lot of energy and so  there's a very small gap at the end of  committing to that to then find your way  out not panic or find the air piece and  i'm a diver i've done this but it's very  different when you're in character  he thinks that the ship he is on is  sinking  we revisit that very pivotal scene in  the middle of the movie where emily  tries to give louis what she thinks he  needs to get through the rest of his  life  the world is full of beauty  and it's up to you to capture it  we really wanted to give you just enough  to long for that moment but also to hold  enough back that you felt shared in that  frustration and shared in that sense of  loss  some of the examples of where we try to  give the audience a glimpse into the  inside of louie's mind  and some of these  memories i suppose you could say  we would shoot we'd project and then  shoot back onto 16 millimeter film  and sometimes it would be clean  processed and often we would use that  for kind of the dreamy more romantic  moments but there are some shots in this  sequence where the film was actually  hand processed james holcomb is one of  the few people who still hand process  his film in the uk  he would treat the film with the  deliberate roughness and kind of scrunch  it up and put acid in the bath  and so it would come back with kind of  deliberately corrupted colours and you  see the sort of synaptic  distressing of the material which  i guess again the